{
  "_comment": "TEMPLATE for tests/manifest.local.json — copy this file to that name (it is gitignored) and replace every <placeholder>. It carries the values that describe YOUR server, YOUR TV and YOUR library; tests/manifest.json carries only the case definitions, which are the same everywhere. run.py merges this over the manifest at load and refuses to run without it. It does NOT refuse over an `items` key it cannot resolve: leave a shape you do not own bracketed and the cases needing it are SKIPPED, with the reason printed in the summary and in --list. Everything else here is still fatal when left as a placeholder, because no run of any size can proceed without it. No secret belongs here either — the X-Plex-Token is still read from the gitignored src/config.local.h at runtime. NB none of this is needed for the DEFAULT tier: a bare `./tests/run.py` plays generated clips served off your own machine, with no PMS, no token and no ratingKey — it needs a TV address and nothing else, and takes it from the repo's gitignored .tv-host if this file does not exist at all. This file is what `./tests/run.py --server` needs. See tests/README.md, 'Two tiers'.",
  "pms": {
    "host": "<pms-host>",
    "port": 32400
  },
  "tv": "<tv-host>",
  "_flavour_comment": "WHICH INSTALL the harness drives: `stable` (com.beb.plxnative, the app users install) or `debug` (com.beb.plxnative.debug, the developer build beside it, with its own launcher tile, its own sign-in and its own runtime files). OPTIONAL -- delete the key and run.py falls back to the Makefile's own default, which is `debug`; --flavor overrides it for one invocation. It decides everything downstream: which app id is closed and launched, which runtime root the triggers and the injected token are written into (/tmp for stable, /tmp/<app id> otherwise), and which event log is graded -- run.py asks the Makefile for each of those (`make -s print-appid/print-rundir/print-eventlog FLAVOR=<f>`) instead of restating them. In practice this stays `debug`: the harness needs a build with the dev triggers compiled in, and `make deploy` refuses to put one on the stable id without ALLOW_DEV_ON_STABLE=1. A run against a release build reads none of its triggers, so it plays nothing and every case fails as if the player were broken -- run.py aborts on the app's `install:` boot line rather than let that happen. Not validated here: the Makefile's FLAVORS whitelist is the one list, and a wrong value fails with its parse-time error naming the allowed set.",
  "flavour": "debug",
  "_test_user_comment": "By default the harness runs as this Plex Home managed user so test playback + timeline scrobbles land on THIS user's history, never the owner's real account. run.py fetches this user's per-server accessToken via the admin token (shared_servers) at runtime -- no secret is stored. `id` is the plex.tv numeric userID, which is what shared_servers is keyed by. Pass --owner to run as the config.local.h owner token instead. The user must already have the libraries shared with it. Delete this block entirely to always run as the owner (run.py warns that watch history WILL be affected).",
  "test_user": {
    "id": "<plex.tv numeric userID>",
    "title": "<display name, cosmetic>"
  },
  "_shared_server_comment": "OPTIONAL, and only for the cases/scenes that declare `needs_shared_server` in tests/manifest.json — a SECOND server, i.e. one a friend shared with your account. It is a separate authority (its own machineIdentifier and its own per-(user,server) accessToken, and your account token gets a 401 from it), so the app needs both credentials at once; run.py writes them to plxnative-servers beside plxnative-token in the install's runtime root. No secret here either: run.py looks the server up in GET https://plex.tv/api/v2/resources with the owner token from src/config.local.h and takes the accessToken plex.tv returns for it. `machine_id` (the server's clientIdentifier) is the match key — `name` alone also works, case-insensitively, but is not stable. `host`/`port` are OPTIONAL: without them run.py ranks the connections plex.tv advertises and prints what it picked. It deliberately does NOT use the app's own sign-in rule, which prefers `local` — for a SHARED server `local` means the OWNER's LAN, not yours (this account's share advertises a `172.20.x.x` that is unroutable from here), so for a non-owned server the public address wins and `local` is demoted below it; within a rank a dotted quad beats a hostname, because the media transport has no DNS; relay is last. Set `host`/`port` explicitly to override that pick. NB watch history: there is no managed-user token for someone else's server, so a case that PLAYS from it plays as YOU on your friend's server. Delete this block entirely if you have no such server — the cases that need it are then skipped, with the reason printed, and the rest of the matrix runs normally.",
  "shared_server": {
    "machine_id": "<the shared server's machineIdentifier / clientIdentifier>",
    "name": "<display name, cosmetic — or the match key if you omit machine_id>",
    "host": "<optional: address the TV can reach it on; omit to use the plex.tv LAN connection>",
    "port": 32400
  },
  "_items_comment": "Maps each symbolic `item` key used by tests/manifest.json to the ratingKey of an item on YOUR server with that shape. The key names the shape the case needs, and each case's `title` in manifest.json spells it out in full, so a missing or wrong mapping is a test-setup error rather than a mystery failure — run.py names the key it could not resolve. Several cases deliberately SHARE one item, which is why run.py resets the server-side resume point before every case. Two keys are for the FPS scenes rather than a playback case and carry an extra requirement: `movie_in_home_catalog` must be reachable from the home catalog (recently-added / on-deck) or detail-transition never enters route=detail, and `movie_cast0_in_both_libraries` must have a first-billed cast member with titles in BOTH libraries so the person page draws two poster shelves. An item whose shape you have nothing for should be LEFT BRACKETED (or deleted): the cases and fps scenes that name it are then skipped by name, with the reason, and everything else runs. Do not map a near-miss to fill the slot -- a case's assertions grade the shape, so an mp4 standing in for the mkv direct-play case fails as if the player had regressed. The suite is a superset of what any one library holds; see tests/README.md, the section on running it against your own library, for what a single ordinary h264/ac3 movie already covers.",
  "items": {
    "movie_h264_ac3_1080p": "<ratingKey>",
    "episode_hevc_4k_hdr10_eac3": "<ratingKey>",
    "episode_hevc_4k_hdr10_eac3_next": "<ratingKey: the episode that follows the one above>",
    "movie_hevc_4k_hdr10_truehd": "<ratingKey>",
    "movie_hevc_4k_dovi_p8": "<ratingKey>",
    "movie_hevc_aac_mp4": "<ratingKey>",
    "episode_h264_aac": "<ratingKey>",
    "movie_h264_ac3_many_audio": "<ratingKey>",
    "movie_av1_no_dp_audio": "<ratingKey>",
    "movie_hevc_4k_pgs_subs": "<ratingKey>",
    "movie_in_home_catalog": "<ratingKey>",
    "movie_cast0_in_both_libraries": "<ratingKey>"
  }
}
